#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba6a29e6-4dab-5854-815c-fa26292c31d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Equilibrium Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a713401e-da23-522b-a10b-86dea4ba2c31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449850} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1249e3-968f-5462-9f9d-e314350bc23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb1249e3-968f-5462-9f9d-e314350bc23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449851} :answer-field/id #uuid "17f69334-b21d-5c28-88c7-e3c5a52c23a5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450879} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b009e278-ea6e-5d9c-8530-bdeaef0a9182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449852} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d40754-d0bf-550f-8098-899ba0c9fb78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b009e278-ea6e-5d9c-8530-bdeaef0a9182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449852} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f33e721-a127-5ee4-a6b7-15e7863d8c52" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450880} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81515a94-dd9d-5006-ab0f-5a8e9330f5f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449854} {:answer/id #uuid "3aad88df-7e50-5b22-9b49-c230cc83c4b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81515a94-dd9d-5006-ab0f-5a8e9330f5f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449854} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fed04d8-7348-5a34-afa4-f4731da87242" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9bb7ec9e-9e63-5c70-a122-192889149887" :question/math-academy-id "q-160444" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+3y+1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y(t)=-1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- **II.** $y(t)=1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- **III.** The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+3y+1.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have that $f(-1)=(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}+3(-1)+1=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have that $f(1)={1}^{3}+3(1)^{2}+3(1)+1=8\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We know that $y=-1$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y+1)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y+1)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y+1)({y}^{2}+2y+1) \\\\  & =(y+1)(y+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(y+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has exactly one equilibrium solution,\n\n$$\ny(t)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95d91c88-80cf-5e60-9a02-2f8a1add5d79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449856} {:answer/id #uuid "c443fbcc-7008-5fcc-8677-7121e62ef09e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c443fbcc-7008-5fcc-8677-7121e62ef09e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449857} :answer-field/id #uuid "b59689df-fb76-51e8-9daf-ad4252596a48" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450882} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86fa2b70-fac9-5247-a29a-0d350830d9e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449858} {:answer/id #uuid "6f690e9b-7ffc-5ad5-83ae-6a067ff174c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f690e9b-7ffc-5ad5-83ae-6a067ff174c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449859} :answer-field/id #uuid "83f06dc6-aaa0-54c5-8f15-50401effd928" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450883} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "992aa33e-1dc4-5291-be9f-c6c9fdfa3acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449860} {:answer/id #uuid "d25be699-e60e-5b9d-8d86-f00138ba80a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d25be699-e60e-5b9d-8d86-f00138ba80a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449861} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab69c169-9c3c-5352-a088-046eca05101b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e574537-84e0-52ce-b8da-3afe1320a385" :question/math-academy-id "q-160459" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{(3x+1)^{4}},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 0$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 0,$ we have $f(x)=\\frac{9}{(3x+1)^{4}}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{(3x+1)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124628 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bf42653-1c16-5ce9-9e7e-a597a3c4c0b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449862} {:answer/id #uuid "e291d1ae-3162-5bd3-9373-07c3fb0e6b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e291d1ae-3162-5bd3-9373-07c3fb0e6b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449863} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfc510c3-d1f2-5891-8a94-d2bea4297462" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450885} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31d951d7-15ec-5f1a-86bd-6bde01c12b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449864} {:answer/id #uuid "e2169951-765d-5b29-a074-703cde25c6fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31d951d7-15ec-5f1a-86bd-6bde01c12b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449864} :answer-field/id #uuid "be3c2c98-dd05-5fd0-8522-655aebe441e6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450886} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7300715e-719a-5c20-b508-764068a2d67f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449866} {:answer/id #uuid "240f47a2-d186-5092-8211-3fe03ff9fda5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7300715e-719a-5c20-b508-764068a2d67f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449866} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac24d236-2e02-5f9e-ab6c-df162685c94e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d50910b7-4560-52e1-8087-133e3875c50e" :question/math-academy-id "q-160861" :question/problem "Let $K$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=-1.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $p(x)\\in K,$ where $p(x)=(x-1)^{3}.$\n\n- **II.** For any $f(x),g(x)\\in K$, we have $f(x)+g(x)\\in K.$\n\n- **III.** The set is closed under addition." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $p(x)=(x-1)^{3}\\in K$ because \n\n$$\np(0)=(0-1)^{3}=(-1)^{3}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exist functions in $K$ such that their sum is *not* in $K.$ For example, let $f(x)=-1$ and $g(x)=(x-1)^{3}.$ Notice that both these functions belong to $K$ since $f(0)=g(0)=-1.$ However,\n\n$$\nf(0)+g(0)=-1+(0-1)^{3}=-2\\ne -1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)+g(x)\\notin K.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278340 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124628 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ae1846d-f710-53e8-94a9-e6c799dde714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449868} {:answer/id #uuid "482f6d32-a3ab-5fb3-a929-84acd8cefcce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "482f6d32-a3ab-5fb3-a929-84acd8cefcce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449869} :answer-field/id #uuid "8231998e-01b3-5f2f-9b76-98785ede333a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450888} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e8fb723-d3f2-5414-b94e-74c3b51047da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449870} {:answer/id #uuid "7b43714b-6bd2-5843-89ae-8b7e89b60abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e8fb723-d3f2-5414-b94e-74c3b51047da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449870} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bc317ea-ab84-541a-a0b2-d8a93778ba55" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450889} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a8d7a21-0c6b-56e1-9665-85586283bc4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449872} {:answer/id #uuid "03fe0f7f-fa95-5286-8155-a88c804a9ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a8d7a21-0c6b-56e1-9665-85586283bc4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449872} :answer-field/id #uuid "9168617c-d949-50fb-a217-849c51506198" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c5fa4f79-ef60-5ce2-8e28-cc585f25eefe" :question/math-academy-id "q-160866" :question/problem "Let $W$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=1.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $q(x)\\in W,$ where $q(x)={x}^{2}+1.$\n- **II.** For any $p(x)\\in W$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot p(x)\\in W.$\n- **III.** The set $W$ is closed under scalar multiplication." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q(x)={x}^{2}+1\\in W$ because\n\n$$\nq(0)=(0)^{2}+1=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a function in $W$ that has a scalar multiple not in $W.$ For example, let $p(x)={x}^{2}+1$ and $k=2.$ Notice that $p(x)$ belongs to $W$ since $p(0)=1.$ However, \n\n$$\n2\\cdot p(0)=2\\cdot ({0}^{2}+1)=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n Hence, $k\\cdot p(x)\\notin W.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278341 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124628 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "051e21cd-15cd-57b6-90e1-60e53a56e03e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449874} {:answer/id #uuid "594bc633-9918-5b51-b0f2-ac79be004a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "594bc633-9918-5b51-b0f2-ac79be004a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449875} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fc70fe9-3a68-5612-afad-71294e56924f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450891} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75517d2c-f45e-5df2-863f-3db4cf4b7df7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449876} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbbf1b5-919a-5559-ab85-bf72f2ea850d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75517d2c-f45e-5df2-863f-3db4cf4b7df7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449876} :answer-field/id #uuid "506358ca-1987-5082-b9b7-0db23b896bd2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450892} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b46510c8-b37a-5e16-9245-1ac6a98ee9fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449878} {:answer/id #uuid "2cd8b2a0-9b38-51bd-901b-612ac12339b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b46510c8-b37a-5e16-9245-1ac6a98ee9fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449878} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e0d358c-f55f-5167-85ce-61aa140bb298" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94165333-3657-56e4-b088-f39008c588be" :question/math-academy-id "q-160872" :question/problem "Let $R$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}],$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${I}_{2}\\in R$\n- **II.** For every $A,B\\in R,$ we have $A+B\\in R.$\n- **III.** The set is closed under addition." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. The identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in R$ since \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwith $a=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There are matrices in $R$ whose sum is *not* in $R.$ For example, let $A=B={I}_{2}.$ Then $A,B\\in R,$  however,\n\n$$\nA+B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\notin R,\n$$\n\nsince the $11$-entry of this resulting matrix is equal to  $2\\ne 1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124628 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93214199-6a10-5339-9647-35cd16bdcedf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449880} {:answer/id #uuid "0149f4d9-a144-51fe-8e20-f590fc7cabb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93214199-6a10-5339-9647-35cd16bdcedf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449880} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd674e35-0795-5636-8fe0-f8882be38364" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450894} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87407cda-57f5-5666-84d6-693f62d4ccca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449882} {:answer/id #uuid "9bcc8b2a-9db9-5815-85e3-20c66b327154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bcc8b2a-9db9-5815-85e3-20c66b327154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449883} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e6f1b71-5eec-58b0-be10-d5087f3cf5ae" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450895} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05711d9f-fe0c-5d6c-924d-02d102e0f888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449884} {:answer/id #uuid "d13fec13-1dcf-51f3-907d-031b92b61292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d13fec13-1dcf-51f3-907d-031b92b61292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449885} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b03f8c9-c106-5a7f-8433-cb73ee72afa3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e68dc301-469b-5da0-9b57-a516f231e45b" :question/math-academy-id "q-160874" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is less than or equal to $2.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $g(x)\\in C,$ where $g(x)={x}^{3}$\n- **II.** For every $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)\\in C.$\n- **III.** The set is closed under addition." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $C$ contains only polynomials whose degree is less than or equal to $2,$ however\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡({x}^{3})=3>2.\n$$\n\nSo, $g(x)={x}^{3}\\notin C.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ then $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p(x))\\le 2$ and $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(q(x))\\le 2.$ Since $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p+q)$ is less than or equal to the maximum of the degrees of $p$ and $q$, we deduce that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p+q)\\le 2.\n$$\n\nHence, $p(x)+q(x)\\in C.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dea8612-414c-5394-869d-1e404b048156" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type III Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1130b77-1288-56bd-9aaa-284154797e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449886} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa762f2-dc0f-5728-9c33-3aaa4b04b087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaa762f2-dc0f-5728-9c33-3aaa4b04b087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449887} :answer-field/id #uuid "87140375-5a1c-5411-8c83-810fa7a04bd4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450897} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "573ba76a-016c-5fd3-a9d5-46c55ef0bae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449888} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e1c800-eff8-51af-a2e1-bce95049cc2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "573ba76a-016c-5fd3-a9d5-46c55ef0bae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449888} :answer-field/id #uuid "1444a723-b137-52b0-acc1-1e143b5421ba" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450898} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c8c4de2-7e16-5521-94ad-1f4111e81460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449890} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1728e6-4f98-525f-86e7-fe2f33ca023b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e1728e6-4f98-525f-86e7-fe2f33ca023b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449891} :answer-field/id #uuid "70643b37-b031-537e-9582-c61eb42df2ec" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a2294020-eb10-5fa0-b942-17d14bfa1ac7" :question/math-academy-id "q-160962" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2x.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(x)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(x),\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}_{1}(x,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=2x$\n- **II.** ${g}_{1}(x)=-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}$ and ${g}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}$\n- **III.** $a=0$ and $b=2$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type III representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z).$ Since all $y$ in the region are bounded *below* by $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and *above* by $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(x,z)}\\le y\\le {{2x}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(x,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2x$ onto the $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $y.$ Substituting this expression for $y$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={x}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $xz$-plane. \n\n![](images/0b/0bcadeca7ede6f709bf06391e10da66afd1b514fd0e041bbf108e6e4229b0da6.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(x)}\\le z\\le {{\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(x)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x.$ From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=0$ and bounded *above* by $x=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{0}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le x\\le {{2}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is true.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133818 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c05d4f9-eb1b-59a1-ae0b-5d6869bad7c5" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type I Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69833de8-8f53-508a-afb8-4cd0942cbfde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449892} {:answer/id #uuid "2e483b85-a74c-551d-91b1-c5d4b4a2b812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69833de8-8f53-508a-afb8-4cd0942cbfde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449892} :answer-field/id #uuid "0110c544-9d0f-5f58-877f-7f806d8b1d46" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450900} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b72e12c2-7cfa-5bf6-8174-edfc888d1b27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449894} {:answer/id #uuid "0da01652-9a03-567e-8945-1d508e3a6b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0da01652-9a03-567e-8945-1d508e3a6b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449895} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd1096c9-f257-5ee9-adb0-c66067e6606c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450901} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a28d2746-3ce7-5e78-9a24-6dc4b1e7349f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449896} {:answer/id #uuid "66c8c5cd-4945-5b7d-8351-ceeb9212d153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66c8c5cd-4945-5b7d-8351-ceeb9212d153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449897} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5d9a367-1c5d-5b35-90d9-1396788916ac" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d909db1e-2d8a-5169-914f-b321d4c2af66" :question/math-academy-id "q-161137" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between  the plane $z=2y+1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$  Given its representation\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- **II.** $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}\\le 4}$\n- **III.** ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2y+1$  and  ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=2y+1$ onto $xy$-plane.  \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2y & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}+2y+1) & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle ${x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}=4$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}\\le 4}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $z$ is \n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2y+1,$ and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278399 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133818 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c05d4f9-eb1b-59a1-ae0b-5d6869bad7c5" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type I Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18631c1a-dc9f-59f0-86c1-a1f4e22e3183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449898} {:answer/id #uuid "e75ba7fe-c065-55c6-b1b7-248c7d69fe11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18631c1a-dc9f-59f0-86c1-a1f4e22e3183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449898} :answer-field/id #uuid "bad823b2-bc47-5409-8d5a-810924dea61c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450903} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3539321c-fba4-56ef-b602-afcc37f1af42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449900} {:answer/id #uuid "a6dc9624-9550-5e8e-a6ac-6b46adf72c76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6dc9624-9550-5e8e-a6ac-6b46adf72c76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449901} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee2f27fe-0d34-5cdf-9eda-b50effcd9fe7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450904} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53d27d94-8283-5c40-b866-9bd4ada708fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449902} {:answer/id #uuid "836bce11-d60e-5dc2-beb2-4d81ce933354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53d27d94-8283-5c40-b866-9bd4ada708fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449902} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcd90bbb-c222-5223-b589-2b9cebe381d6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "91da0da4-8cfa-5b1f-811f-ea50a1876fb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-161143" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between  the plane $z=2y+1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ Given its representation\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(y)\\le x\\le {g}_{2}(y),\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}_{1}(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=2y+1$\n- **II.** ${g}_{1}(y)=-\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}$ and ${g}_{2}(y)=\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}$\n- **III.** $a=1$ and $b=2$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type I representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,y)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y).$ Since all $z$ in the region are bounded *below* by $z=2y+1$ and *above* by $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n{{2y+1}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(x,y)}\\le z\\le {{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(x,y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=2y+1$ onto the $xy$-plane. \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2y & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}+2y+1) & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle ${x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}=4$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.    \n\n![](images/39/39d2daceb171d26d3396e5aaeab4d8b8da2e9b8324e4042f2b7ed048f4a5fdeb.png)\n\nThe region is bounded on the *left* by $x=-\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}$ and bounded on the *right* by $x=\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,      \n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le x\\le {{\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=-3$ and bounded *above* by $y=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-3}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{1}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133818 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c05d4f9-eb1b-59a1-ae0b-5d6869bad7c5" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type I Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dcc3ead-a5eb-59d5-b395-84368e02a2fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449904} {:answer/id #uuid "552b29d1-81b4-522d-a1e5-9344bd4cad3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "552b29d1-81b4-522d-a1e5-9344bd4cad3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449905} :answer-field/id #uuid "91461d3a-7bce-5e80-91c1-dce4ac1cc87d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450906} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a02cc7a9-2caf-5794-bb5c-7df397d6341f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449906} {:answer/id #uuid "69bf2c68-8883-5ae2-9be1-422c63c83c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a02cc7a9-2caf-5794-bb5c-7df397d6341f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449906} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c9d4940-8b58-5e01-be4b-3c772cf8c4ce" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450907} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17d163fe-da7f-55c1-99a3-81e1b07d0123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449908} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b0d5a4-2df0-5a30-9edb-eaa989bafb4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17d163fe-da7f-55c1-99a3-81e1b07d0123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449908} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3ab31af-0692-56bd-b3dc-792901c29841" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e71e8fdb-04f6-527e-89b1-2171dc56e3a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-161160" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $z=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=-2x-2y+6.$ Given its representation\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 4}$\n- **II.** $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2}$\n- **III.** ${f}_{1}(x,y)=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2x-2y+6$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=-2x-2y+6$ onto $xy$-plane. \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-2y+6 & =8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}-2y & =2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+({y}^{2}-2y+1) & =4 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=4$ centered at $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 4}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that $z$ is \n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,y)=-2x-2y+6,$ and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,y)=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dea8612-414c-5394-869d-1e404b048156" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type III Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a794a4f8-da20-5971-8a90-3a6d7680175b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449910} {:answer/id #uuid "0ddc61b0-b1b8-57ae-a1f6-e691bde0a52e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a794a4f8-da20-5971-8a90-3a6d7680175b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449910} :answer-field/id #uuid "be1dc7db-5a90-5ac6-b16c-3476af279190" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450909} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ab18d4-5add-53fc-a4f1-49309f43d817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449912} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef13aae-9d06-55e8-aa97-0b13a4e8888e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ef13aae-9d06-55e8-aa97-0b13a4e8888e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449913} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d1f0558-c605-5b82-8cc6-62553a4e58a7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450910} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "089b8d5f-df8d-5f82-b2b6-069b93905ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449914} {:answer/id #uuid "85696183-ea90-5370-a383-c83e17dff64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85696183-ea90-5370-a383-c83e17dff64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449915} :answer-field/id #uuid "895d4087-1996-5d9e-a521-0e3cb5ce834c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae22adfc-61f1-5211-b117-6cf267aa0468" :question/math-academy-id "q-161190" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2z+1.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,(2x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- **II.** $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2}\\le 2}$\n- **III.** ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=2z+1$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. First, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2z+1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $y.$ Substituting this expression for $y$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z+1 & =2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-2z-1 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+({z}^{2}-2z+1) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2}=1$ centered at $(0,1)$ in the $xz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the interior part of the following ellipse:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2}\\le 2}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $y$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,z)=2z+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dea8612-414c-5394-869d-1e404b048156" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type III Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0fdc915-2f4f-5be8-ace6-d347fb2f4865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449916} {:answer/id #uuid "e00ae6d8-020c-528c-ba04-246cf66b857e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e00ae6d8-020c-528c-ba04-246cf66b857e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449917} :answer-field/id #uuid "260c3d22-1e9f-5906-9107-1b9c02819237" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450912} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e17f328e-2edb-5810-9d94-d51cf2a49790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449918} {:answer/id #uuid "b8cc3cc6-e5f0-5f92-b79b-c43f1acec5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e17f328e-2edb-5810-9d94-d51cf2a49790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449918} :answer-field/id #uuid "77a70ca3-7a52-5b13-9518-39bf41505ce8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450913} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0874913e-527d-5ba6-9f78-dfdcb226fe66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449920} {:answer/id #uuid "36b546ec-cfc7-5682-84a0-926cc25d8896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36b546ec-cfc7-5682-84a0-926cc25d8896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449921} :answer-field/id #uuid "b416ad05-b4e0-5f61-b941-be8dbb99929c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d04b955c-6599-5f09-8a8d-748ca0ca3061" :question/math-academy-id "q-161225" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}\\le 3}$\n- **II.** $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}\\le 2}$\n- **III.** ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. First, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equations are already solved for $y.$ Equating the two expressions, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1 & =2{x}^{2}+2z-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2z-2 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+({z}^{2}+2z+1) & =3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=1$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the interior part of the ellipse:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}\\le 3}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $y$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$, and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,z)=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dea8612-414c-5394-869d-1e404b048156" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type III Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4f131be-9aa0-5bc3-ae76-2eb8786f9d33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449922} {:answer/id #uuid "e797117f-70c6-557d-bd1e-cbb4972747af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e797117f-70c6-557d-bd1e-cbb4972747af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449923} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5069733-839b-5a49-a7a9-a29335ca99a8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450915} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fff03229-c2a9-50c3-be3d-bed8ece6abe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449924} {:answer/id #uuid "15c7ceb4-b34d-5bbe-8b3d-91c9440e5a01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fff03229-c2a9-50c3-be3d-bed8ece6abe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449924} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb73a07c-0a5e-50a2-a801-7db3e31249e5" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450916} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be2629f8-d736-527c-8142-c79651c88560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449926} {:answer/id #uuid "6d74fb52-0df1-54d2-9b79-aac32987bb61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be2629f8-d736-527c-8142-c79651c88560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449926} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c60454e-8a50-5bb0-a967-d107eee3f120" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2955e9f2-2674-539d-b301-d0ca76c12950" :question/math-academy-id "q-161227" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(x)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(x),\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$\n- **II.** ${g}_{1}(x)=-\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}$ and ${g}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}$\n- **III.** $a=0$ and $b=1$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type III representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z).$ Since all $y$ in the region are bounded *below* by $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ and *above* by $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{2{x}^{2}+2z-1}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(x,z)}\\le y\\le {{-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(x,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equations are already solved for $y.$ Equating the two expressions, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1 & =2{x}^{2}+2z-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2z-2 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+({z}^{2}+2z+1) & =3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=3$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xz$-plane. \n\n![](images/86/868fceefed2a426feebe2a9199279826aaee935963fec8fa3761b17b97542c34.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-1-\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=-1+\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-1-\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(x)}\\le z\\le {{-1+\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(x)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x.$ From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=-1$ and bounded *above* by $x=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-1}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le x\\le {{1}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcc1bf5-5eea-5615-8b16-b8ced5de7984" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7d665b5-198a-55b9-8bf0-26a0989fd40d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449928} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc21ed5-3473-5160-9a7c-f60d49ba1004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7d665b5-198a-55b9-8bf0-26a0989fd40d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449928} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf3cc37a-5965-531e-af36-32f1bfafcf65" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450918} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f70b2733-9448-5ef8-9fed-f80eee91840f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449930} {:answer/id #uuid "b106c40c-33c4-5685-9951-2719c69fd8b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b106c40c-33c4-5685-9951-2719c69fd8b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449931} :answer-field/id #uuid "fadb5115-6f6b-58f5-8341-276905f31336" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450919} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23016ffc-39e9-5547-82ce-1b84aa5a0183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449932} {:answer/id #uuid "92f2e35f-57e5-558a-885d-c46d619b49b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92f2e35f-57e5-558a-885d-c46d619b49b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449933} :answer-field/id #uuid "26a23ac2-b6e0-583e-afa8-aef987afd7b2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62e7d4a5-ede7-555e-89aa-1ccf67e4530f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161380" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the parabolic cylinder $x=\\sqrt{2y}.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- **II.** $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- **III.** ${f}_{1}(y,z)=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\sqrt{2y}$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/98/98b9dc84e3d23d43c1fef204109d6f6b6c571d6f188592f1f08bb1abf4dc3641.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, and statement II is true. \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the paraboloid $x=\\sqrt{2y}$ onto $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the paraboloid is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the cone, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{2y} \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+1+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $x$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(y,z)=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},$ and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\sqrt{2y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcc1bf5-5eea-5615-8b16-b8ced5de7984" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d47a24e-a556-5ced-ba9f-3c650813c6f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449934} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a9e2e0-aed2-5091-979e-21ff7c9eba24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8a9e2e0-aed2-5091-979e-21ff7c9eba24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449935} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e5d2a03-b9e9-5f0c-a3ec-d16a44ca37a2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450921} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cee72eca-362e-5787-88b7-034467acdb24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449936} {:answer/id #uuid "43765983-0124-5795-a220-df09cf706cc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cee72eca-362e-5787-88b7-034467acdb24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449936} :answer-field/id #uuid "c84360ce-bf39-55dc-bc78-8243b8db69d8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450922} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a0c394-f144-5433-850a-9baa59daa498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449938} {:answer/id #uuid "0a7f24e0-7cfa-5706-831b-1bb82e855622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a7f24e0-7cfa-5706-831b-1bb82e855622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449939} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5938a9e-a410-5cc4-8d1d-82dada2ccbd0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0a480e12-228f-5db7-81b6-a3f28cbc1181" :question/math-academy-id "q-161386" :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between  the elliptic paraboloid $x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}$ and the plane $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(y)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(y),\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}_{1}(y,z)=\\frac{1}{4}$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}$\n- **II.** ${g}_{1}(y)=-\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}$ and ${g}_{2}(y)=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}$\n- **III.** $a=-3$ and $b=3$" :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ and *above* by $x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16},$ we have\n\n$$\n{{\\frac{1}{4}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le {{\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}$ and the plane $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$ centered at $(0,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](images/46/46757c2bf93af4916afbd019f0faed2eef25245e987acbeff7eafb3e0ca7cd8c.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le {{4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=-3$ and bounded *above* by $y=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-3}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{3}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is true.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24d1e79b-e7fb-5978-8b61-317e3cdc2318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449940} {:answer/id #uuid "bc77f129-c1ae-5fbc-bc29-dc39b9e0d483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc77f129-c1ae-5fbc-bc29-dc39b9e0d483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449941} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ad42456-eefd-513b-b1ff-1ddbbb8c3215" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450924} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee14783-2c54-554a-85a4-909bd52480c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449942} {:answer/id #uuid "57213e39-07f9-53b0-9e6a-2235cede2a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ee14783-2c54-554a-85a4-909bd52480c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449942} :answer-field/id #uuid "43ad0e94-a683-565d-a041-fe8827a078c0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450925} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cdc4b8b-a4b8-5f63-9a52-137f8257c0db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449944} {:answer/id #uuid "ff06a87a-6dcf-5b24-936f-02500a3aa3ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff06a87a-6dcf-5b24-936f-02500a3aa3ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449945} :answer-field/id #uuid "b03afc6c-2900-515e-85f5-539a9b27b138" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2307ae9e-a249-5090-bfed-df0660f7b0eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161601" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<36\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n\n- **II.** $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n\n- **III.** $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/ff/ff1f735266f66879834e02ca7601e90e40dfa7929e5796d4a3f8cb4ff2828de7.png)\n\n  \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A=A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be55a3c3-a14a-5616-b434-748a64016ec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449946} {:answer/id #uuid "9b71986f-ed36-5a4e-9a5f-7dc9b8a49066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b71986f-ed36-5a4e-9a5f-7dc9b8a49066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449947} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0acb8a6-343b-54a7-9d7f-399cf2145053" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450927} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d69f8359-c7e9-5292-948c-328d26d2fcd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449948} {:answer/id #uuid "947dbbdb-26b6-5c81-b11b-1ddb925d5b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d69f8359-c7e9-5292-948c-328d26d2fcd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449948} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a149c98-887c-59d2-92f6-de50cb110a9c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450928} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9b547fe-4e7e-53cd-bca0-9119bfd32cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449950} {:answer/id #uuid "69ad3907-4b5f-5519-bfd6-a21aa64633f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9b547fe-4e7e-53cd-bca0-9119bfd32cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449950} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee22d64e-0f8b-5b04-928a-65a44a0afe68" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4104ec23-658c-58b7-8dbf-72086dab259b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161622" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge -1+x}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n- **II.** $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n- **III.** $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3e/3e7d8c11ac4b3de8473741a362ad2b78555c764da2d61f955435e8d59771cf46.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ contains all of its boundary points, we must have $A-∂A⊊A.$ Therefore $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34114c44-99df-5eb6-abf1-299e7f8a59e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449952} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a7d464-f27e-5816-910d-97e3198e8ac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34114c44-99df-5eb6-abf1-299e7f8a59e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449952} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8c5a9d2-ddb3-5ced-9423-63314912bd6c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450930} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d7ec702-57a1-5c3e-8b98-8dd92e48f1f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449954} {:answer/id #uuid "9b3e8b1f-44eb-5090-ac11-cc11a79bc546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b3e8b1f-44eb-5090-ac11-cc11a79bc546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449955} :answer-field/id #uuid "a05a88b2-59a5-5a9b-8d98-69da3d4daec3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450931} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d950cf8-fba5-5439-a346-fb6f6919aabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449956} {:answer/id #uuid "f65576ec-dbd6-5f03-8c29-7a62088cf6b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d950cf8-fba5-5439-a346-fb6f6919aabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449956} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef878e5f-c50a-5783-85f5-385663e493c3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35b15061-fc8b-55f3-8742-4ca105208a91" :question/math-academy-id "q-161624" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n\n- **II.** $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n\n- **III.** $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/84/8414de2e05da43c03fae3c8b1ff2102401e990188e9fd4ee59a24308ec34b086.png)\n\n \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. As we have seen, $∂A$ consists of two concentric circles: the outer one is in $A,$ while the inner one is not.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ contains some of its boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5b39a98-ac20-5cbf-805a-5c64b48f4d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449958} {:answer/id #uuid "a6123e03-9f1a-55ae-8049-e66f7df48abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6123e03-9f1a-55ae-8049-e66f7df48abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449959} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb5b9ea3-b19f-572e-90a5-93f3f3d18280" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450933} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc09b043-a237-5147-8e74-3cb2d48c0db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449960} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f5341e-9ebd-53a8-b977-c25f8d8c2d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc09b043-a237-5147-8e74-3cb2d48c0db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449960} :answer-field/id #uuid "45b72198-7edb-5c3f-a56e-f84b68b732be" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450934} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e755d2a-b5e6-5e96-88fd-d0e6cd0cfc47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449962} {:answer/id #uuid "896e016a-452b-53dc-9c84-3690b98756c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "896e016a-452b-53dc-9c84-3690b98756c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449963} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b80112e-c8cb-5761-852a-1dc1da395e6b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "baee2889-195d-5ff5-b660-0a9acac1604c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161626" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}<4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n- **II.** $A-\\text{int}\\,A=∂A$\n- **III.** $∂A⊂{R}^{2}-A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/45/4540e03fc5404474a0cd900c89004e6a5cfe7ac2d4702348e9d0395fc1c914e9.png)\n\n \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.  \n\n![](images/2a/2a93b912f0d7b3a84bf0ec9c3b3596d080a9e71a345ff436e2f591edf042d9db.png)\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $A=\\text{int}\\,A$ or, equivalently,  $A-\\text{int}\\,A=∅.$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊂{R}^{2}-A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and  III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1e2b183-1fbb-5d3a-95b8-ba5907b77381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449964} {:answer/id #uuid "009859f9-d5f0-5f12-8808-874a29b0d49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186